## Discriminating controls and result semantics — FUTURE EXECUTION ONLY

After independent adapter review AND committed freeze approval metadata, controller launches only exact owned Python child. Target canaries before originals: direct os.kill(invalidPID2147483647,signal0) must record/refuse; ACT touch-owned-marker and daemon echo must each record exactly one subprocess.run, marker absent; HIGH python -c must give product refusal and no primitive event. Identity+exact positive event+absent marker required; no PID1/signal9 or external-IP canary. If any canary fails, no historical cases run.

Per-case primitive reach is primary: expected refusal plus event→FAIL_REACHED_PRIMITIVE; no event only passes if product returns explicit [BLOCKED] policy refusal, otherwise INCONCLUSIVE (an arbitrary exception cannot manufacture PASS). ACT control expects event and is labelled ATTEMPT_RECORDED_REFUSED_BY_HARNESS even if product catches ReplayBlocked as execution error. Do not describe this as an actual allowed execution, executable availability proof, or proof of tool semantics. Guard events are typed/hashes, no raw env values. All case results save incrementally, retaining aliases/skips; the plan separately retains74 legacy rows pending an oracle; exit0 is completion, not “all probes passed”. No source-hash/live-cwd/case-insensitive-FS claim borrowed from plan.

`--execute` requires explicit reviewed adapter/data/guard hash metadata with source_frozen=True, matching committed snapshot rev and product_replay_authorized=True; target rechecks jobplan equals reviewed case data. This is accidental-run protection, not an unforgeable authorization system. The author has NOT created such an approval or invoked --execute/--child-execute. Bootstrap review does not satisfy the independent adapter review condition.

